![](/images/articles/founder-mode.jpeg)

The legendary Paul Graham[ provoked](https://email.joinpavilion.com/calling-bs-on-founder-mode)[ strong](https://www.inc.com/joe-procopio/why-your-founder-mode-ceo-is-terrible.html)[ reactions](https://kellblog.com/2024/09/07/we-are-not-the-same-the-obligatory-post-on-founder-mode/)[ to](https://www.inc.com/sara-deeter/heres-what-happens-when-women-do-founder-mode-how-they-can-navigate-backlash.html)[ his](https://tidyfirst.substack.com/p/the-founder-mode-tradeoff)[ essay on Founder Mode](https://paulgraham.com/foundermode.html). I am ill-suited to respond; I lack the imagination, audacity, and risk tolerance to be a founder. I can be accused of bias, since I suppose I am what Paul refers to as a “merely a professional manager.” But being poorly qualified to make an argument has never stopped me before. I’m eager to question some of Paul’s conclusions, but also to join his celebration of great founders.

We must first dispense, however, with some of the breathless adoration of a Founder Class. When you talk to a group of founders whose companies screwed up, do not be surprised to find that *it wasn’t their fault, and if only they had done things their way,* things would have been great.

We must also get past the Syllogism of Founder Infallibility, in which if everyone is telling you you’re right, you’re probably right, but if everyone is telling you you’re wrong, you’re probably still right, because you’re a Founder.

But there *are* clear mechanisms through which founders yield special power. They deeply understand the values and principles that make their companies special. They carry moral authority to create change that no one else can; they can challenge old assumptions, discard sacred cows, and pivot the business with speed in ways few professional managers can match.

Never model yourself on an outlier. Imitate Michael Jordan’s tongue-out drive to the basket and your reward will be a bloody tongue. Imitating Steve Jobs to try to be like Steve Jobs will probably just make you an asshole who’s not a genius like Steve Jobs.[ Or land you in prison](https://apnews.com/article/elizabeth-holmes-theranos-prison-fraud-texas-71bcdf58a0db73252cc2697fb9f73c8a). But if we are going to inspect Steve’s status as a founder, then let’s do it right.

When Steve began his second act at Apple in 1997, he restored a deep understanding of what made the company special. The elevation of design, the humanizing of technology through whimsy, surprise, and delight, the focus on user values, the celebration of creativity — this is what brought Apple back to greatness. And Steve’s status as Founder meant not only that he deeply understood this, but that he wielded the moral authority to restore these values, in part by ruthlessly slashing projects and focusing on a famously narrow set of products. When he realized that his vision needed a new buying experience, he built Apple Retail. And his status as Founder gave him moral authority to pursue this great decision that, indeed, virtually everyone else thought was dumb.

But of central importance to this story is that to build Apple Retail, he brought in a “professional manager” — Ron Johnson from Target. And while Steve was the spark for Apple Retail, it was Steve’s partnership with Ron that shaped its success.

Indeed, it is exactly the partnerships with the “professional managers” that in many ways distinguishes Steve’s second act. The young Steve had recruited John Sculley, and that relationship was not (to state the obvious) a success. But with hindsight, we realize it had no right to be. Whatever Sculley’s strengths as a manager might have been, 15 years marketing Pepsi products left him woefully and predictably unprepared to be a tech CEO. And Jobs was still a young, petulant know-it-all (you only get to be called “mercurial” when you’re older and an acknowledged genius) who surely did *not* give Sculley the room to do his job.

Contrast this with Jobs’ second act, when he recruited not only Ron Johnson but Tim Cook. Even since Steve’s death, Tim has not tried to be Steve. And Steve, from everything I can tell, never tried to be Tim either. This was a true partnership between a product genius who could infuse Apple’s devices with his values and an operations genius who could make Apple a great business.

Consider other legendary companies of Silicon Valley that follow a similar pattern. Eric Schmidt with Sergey Brin and Larry Page at Google. Sheryl Sandberg with Mark Zuckerberg at Facebook.

Nor are these the only models of success. Meg Whitman was not a founder at eBay. Nor Elon Musk at Tesla. Nor Andy Grove at Intel. Nor John Chambers at Cisco. Yet each of them became the defining leaders of their companies.

The real world is more complex than the divide between the heroic Founder Class that Paul celebrates, and the charlatans and liars in the Professional Manager class that he seems to despise. Lost is the observation that the Founders were the ones who *hired* the managers, and perhaps bear some level of accountability for the myriad things that can go wrong whenever you hire anyone. Or that, with less than one in six venture-backed companies making it to a C round of funding, some Founders could use a little help.

Some professional managers are, doubtless, the skillful liars Paul proclaims them to be. But even the best “professional managers” aren’t suited to every job. John Sculley was spectacularly successful at Pepsi, but not at Apple. Ron Johnson was legendary at Apple, but utterly imploded at JCPenney.

I frequently reference what I call the Anna Karenina rule: All successful companies are alike; each unsuccessful company is unsuccessful in its own way. The successful companies are *not* all alike in having Founders who ignore and dismiss professional managers. The successful companies recruit great people and leverage their strengths and passions. Sometimes, of course, that requires the best founders to do what they did to found their companies in the first place – to dare to be different, to buck conventional wisdom, to challenge assumptions — to *disrupt*. But we all stand on the shoulders of giants, and no one builds a great company alone.

I celebrate the great founders that Paul Graham reveres. And he has forgotten more since breakfast about founding and funding companies than I’ll ever know.

But the record suggests that our greatest founders achieved their highest success when they neither deferred to nor dismissed the professional managers that Paul seems to hold in such disdain. Rather, the greatest founders achieved their highest success when they leveraged their strengths, their understanding, and their moral authority, and partnered with business leaders who complemented their strengths.
